    \u0444\u0440\u0430\u0437\u0430 \u00abAnother roof, another proof\u00bb (\u00ab\u0415\u0449\u0435
    \u043e\u0434\u043d\u0430 \u043a\u0440\u044b\u0448\u0430 \u043d\u0430\u0434 \u0433\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0432\u043e\u0439,
    \u0435\u0449\u0435 \u043e\u0434\u043d\u043e \u0434\u043e\u043a\u0430\u0437\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u0441\u0442\u0432\u043e\u00bb)
    \u0441\u0442\u0430\u043b\u0430 \u043a\u0440\u044b\u043b\u0430\u0442\u043e\u0439 \u0432 \u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043c\u0430\u0442\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u043e\u043c
    \u043c\u0438\u0440\u0435, \u043e\u0442\u0440\u0430\u0436\u0430\u044f \u0435\u0433\u043e
    \u0436\u0438\u0437\u043d\u044c \u043a\u0430\u043a \u0447\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0434\u044b \u0431\u0435\u0441\u043a\u043e\u043d\u0435\u0447\u043d\u044b\u0445
    \u043f\u0443\u0442\u0435\u0448\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0438\u0439 \u0438 \u0441\u043e\u0432\u043c\u0435\u0441\u0442\u043d\u044b\u0445
    \u0440\u0430\u0431\u043e\u0442."
  interestingFactsEn: "The life of Pal Erdös was so unusual that even the simplest moments of the home were anecdotes and legends, and behind his eccentricity was a deep philosophy that made mathematics not just a science, but a way of life.\n\nErdöszew: The Superme Fascist, SF: The secret code of genius Erdös spoke in his unique language, which shocked colleagues and made his speech similar to the cipher: \"The Supreme Fascist\" (The Supreme Fascist, SF): That's what he called God. Erdösh was convinced that God deliberately hid from people the most beautiful mathematical evidence and, of course, his Hungarian passport at the most inappropriate time.\"The Book\" (The Book): This is a thought-based collection of the most elegant and perfect evidence that is stored in the Upper Fascist. When Erdös saw a particularly beautiful solution, he said, \"This is from the Book!\" \"Epsilon\": that's what he called children. In the math of Epsilon (`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\) is infinitely small, and Erdösh believed that children are as tiny as adults. \"Boss: Women. \"Rabs: Men. \"Shum\": Music that prevented him from thinking.\"The poison: alcohol.\" \"Sam\" and \"Joe\": That's what he called the US and the Soviet Union to avoid possible problems with the authorities during the cold war. When Erdösh came to his colleagues, he could tell the housewife, \"Boss, we're hungry. When the slaves will eat?\", what usually led others to the stand.\n\nThe Erdös was a legendary workaholic. He could work 18 to 20 hours a day, and his main weapon was amphetamines and coffee (up to 10 cups a day). He didn't think it was an addiction, but he called it a \"needs\" -- the most indicative case was in 1979 -- his friend, the mathematician Ron Graham, argued with him for $500 that Erdösh wouldn't live a month without amphetamine.Erdös accepted the challenge and really stood up for 30 days, but when he received the money, he was sad to say, \"You proved me not a drug addict, but this month mathematician was thrown back. I sat at the table looking at empty paper and didn't see the theorem, just an empty sheet.\" Immediately after that, he took a dosage and went back to work. His famous phrase, \"Mathematics is a machine to turn coffee into theorems\", became the motto of his life.And he was so unattractive that he ate everything he gave and slept on the floor if there was no bed.\n\nThe generosity of the mad Erdös scale made no difference to money except for the opportunity to help others. The Wolf Award: In 1984, he received a prestigious $50,000 prize. Erdös kept exactly $720 (the minimum amount for legal entry into the United States), and the rest donated to the establishment of a scholarship for his parents' name in Israel and to help elderly mathematicians.The prizes for hypotheses: Erdös often announced monetary awards ($25 to $10,000) for solving his mathematical problems, but he wrote checks in the name of the person who solved the problem, but often, without changing the signatures, handed the checks to other people as a gift. Sometimes he gave all the cash he had in his pocket to the first student he met or to the beggar. \"Man without things\": All his luggage consisted of an old, half-empty suitcase.He couldn't tie his shoelaces, he couldn't use his shower (he could only wash in the bathtub), but he once tried to open a bag of tomato juice by stabbing him with a knife, and was shocked when the juice spilled.\n\nWhen he was four years old, he himself \"opened\" negative numbers. He once said to his mother, \"If you take from four apples 6, you'll have minus two apples.\" But that's absurd!\", and when you're talking about negative values, he didn't like beauty: he, unlike many mathematicians, wasn't interested in the aesthetics of the tasks.He said, \"If there's a beautiful proof and ugly proof of one theorem, choose an ugly one if it's easier.\" \"The death of civilization\": He was ironic about the modern world. For example, the day before his death, a ticket to a conference in Vilnius was found in his pocket, but there was also a note in his notebook: \"The death of civilization was 17.04.2026, 17.00\" (he often joked about the end of the world in 2026, but no one took it seriously).\n\nThe most absurd fact is that the King of the Netherlands is perhaps the most amazing fact of his life story. Erdös was a Jew and a refugee, but he had a unique connection with the Dutch royal family. In the 1960s, Queen Julian of the Netherlands, being a deeply religious man, was interested in mathematics. She invited Erdös to her palace and asked her to teach her math.Erdös agreed, but he made the conditions: he would not tolerate pass, laziness, and negligence. He would come to the palace and give the queen assignments. When he was not satisfied with her success, he would say to her, \"Iliana, you think like an idiot.\" And the Queen, instead of being offended, would sit down and overdo the task. They say that it was because of her studying with Erdösh that she became one of Europe's most educated monarchs.\n\nThe legacy of the art of Erdös was such a coloured figure that he became a hero of documentary films and books, and his image was immortalized even in music. In a play by the American dramatist Robert Schneider \"Erdös\", he was portrayed as a Bible prophet who had gone down into the modern world.His famous phrase \"Another roof, another proof\" became a wing in the mathematical world, reflecting his life as a series of endless journeys and joint works."
